`,r.jsxs(e.p,{children:["The assignment ",r.jsx(e.code,{children:"cmnd = buf"})," changes only the function's local pointer. It makes ",r.jsx(e.code,{children:"cmnd"})," refer to the canonical path, such as ",r.jsx(e.code,{children:"/usr/bin/true"}),", while ",r.jsx(e.code,{children:"ctx->user.cmnd"})," still contains the submitted path, such as ",r.jsx(e.code,{children:"/home/low/switch/true"}),"."]}),`
`,r.jsxs(e.p,{children:["The vulnerable assumption is captured by the final comment. A regular-expression match does not mean that the two pathname strings are identical, and in the fallback case they are specifically different. Because the function returns success without copying the checked ",r.jsx(e.code,{children:"cmnd"})," into ",r.jsx(e.code,{children:"ctx->runas.cmnd"}),", the execution stage can fall back to ",r.jsx(e.code,{children:"ctx->user.cmnd"}),"."]}),`
`,r.jsxs(e.p,{children:[r.jsx(e.code,{children:"set_cmnd_fd()"})," does not automatically close this gap in the demonstrated default configuration. With the default ",r.jsx(e.code,{children:"fdexec=digest_only"})," setting and no digest in the rule, ",r.jsx(e.code,{children:"open_cmnd()"})," does not retain an executable descriptor. The result is a successful policy decision with the original mutable pathname still selected for execution."]}),`
