`,r.jsx(e.p,{children:"What I actually spend my time on:"}),`
`,r.jsxs(e.ul,{children:[`
`,r.jsx(e.li,{children:"Automating the boring bits of offensive and defensive work, so the interesting bits get more time"}),`
`,r.jsx(e.li,{children:"Pushing security testing earlier in the SDLC without making developers hate it"}),`
`,r.jsx(e.li,{children:"Securing AI and LLM systems, from the features users see down to the infrastructure they run on"}),`
`,r.jsx(e.li,{children:"Independent vulnerability research and responsible disclosure"}),`
`,r.jsx(e.li,{children:"Detection engineering around the build and deploy pipeline"}),`
`]}),`
`,r.jsx(e.p,{children:"The offensive and defensive sides of my work feed into each other. Exploit development changes what I look for in a code review. Threat modelling gives me new questions to investigate. Building security tooling is a way to take something learned from one application and make it useful for the next."}),`
`,r.jsx(e.p,{children:"Outside my day-to-day work, I make time for independent research and hands-on learning. I like digging into unfamiliar systems, working out why something behaves the way it does, and sharing the parts that might save someone else a few hours down the same rabbit hole."}),`
